# Line endings are normalized to LF in the repository and checked out as LF on
# every platform, Windows included. Do not "fix" a file that shows CRLF in a
# Windows editor: that is the working tree lying to you, and these rules are
# what stop it from reaching a commit.
#
# A Windows agent writing files must use LF explicitly -- see the line-ending
# rules in conventions.md. These attributes are the backstop, not the plan.

# Default: text files are stored as LF and come back as LF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The case that fails silently and far from its cause: a CRLF shell script
# runs on Windows and dies in WSL with a syntax error on the wrong line.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Windows-native formats keep CRLF in the working tree. The repository copy is
# still LF, so a diff across platforms stays readable.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf
*.reg text eol=crlf

# Never touch these.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
